  "body": "## Scope\n\nAdd a public `camcraft_post::agie_setup::PunchSetup20::encode` function. A Rust caller can pair its complete SBL piece script and SBR resource manifest with an existing ISO file. The caller supplies a program name, stock bounds and the position of the 2 mm threading hole in micrometres.\n\nThe encoder supports one closed punch in 20 mm steel 0001 with Berco 0.25 mm wire, Ra 0.30 and 1 mm separation. It binds the requested native quality and removes the erosion steps on import. The returned review text gives the expected offsets for the operator. The API does not generate, position or verify the caller's ISO contour. The caller must use the matching resource and check it in the control.\n\nThis branch starts directly from main at c5f4e6f94. It has no feature-stack commits, no new dependencies and no automatic machining option. The new function works independently of the CAMCraft workspace post. The first workspace writer must call this function and report the controller review before it can ship. The existing feature drafts need their own safety reviews.\n\n## References\n\nThe MIT-licensed Agie Processor implementation supplied the Agie-specific interface reference. The contributor's team wrote that implementation. The license notice is in assets/ui/LICENSES.md. The two synthetic expected files come from the contributor's control-checked CCSEP20 example.\n\n## Verification\n\nThe complete quick gate passed all 107 steps in 3.1 minutes on the final head befdc57f9. The complete reached heavy gate passed all 197 steps in 64.3 minutes. These commands also passed on that head:\n\n```sh\ncargo xtask gate quick --base c5f4e6f94\ncargo xtask gate heavy --base c5f4e6f94\ncargo test -p camcraft-post --test agie_safe_setup\ncargo test -p camcraft-post --doc agie_setup\ncargo clippy -p camcraft-post --all-targets -- -D warnings\n```\n\nAll four integration tests passed. The public API example compiled and ran. An independent Rust caller reproduced the complete CCSEP20.SBL and CCSEP20.SBR files byte for byte. On a copy of the actual offline control, the new piece had zero working steps. FORMALCHECK required the operator to define the technology. The work showed the intended punch, ISO, stock height and threading hole.\n\nThe pinning tests are:\n\n- `setup_files_match_the_control_checked_zero_step_punch`\n- `a_named_resource_and_signed_micron_coordinates_round_trip`\n- `device_names_paths_and_script_text_cannot_enter_an_agie_record`\n- `stock_and_full_start_hole_must_fit_the_setup_span`\n\nNo physical erosion was performed.\n\nEffort: high\nChangelog: The post library can encode a zero-step Agie setup for a qualified 20 mm punch with a caller-prepared ISO contour.\n\nRefs #2984\n\n",
